1
00:00:00,280 --> 00:00:03,092
Today we meet the distributive property of whole numbers.

2
00:00:03,092 --> 00:00:05,789
We will also meet two very special numbers: zero

3
00:00:05,789 --> 00:00:10,611
and one. Let's begin with a multiplication puzzle!

4
00:00:11,307 --> 00:00:13,567
Here are twenty dots, arranged in five rows of

5
00:00:13,567 --> 00:00:17,147
four. So five times four is twenty. Now watch

6
00:00:17,147 --> 00:00:21,067
carefully: we draw a line after the second row.

7
00:00:21,067 --> 00:00:23,602
The rows move apart, but not a single dot

8
00:00:23,602 --> 00:00:26,912
has been added or taken away. Can two smaller

9
00:00:26,912 --> 00:00:28,538
groups give the same total?

10
00:00:29,200 --> 00:00:31,753
Let's count each group. The first group has two

11
00:00:31,753 --> 00:00:34,172
rows of four, so two times four is eight.

12
00:00:34,172 --> 00:00:36,806
The second group has three rows of four, so

13
00:00:36,806 --> 00:00:40,120
three times four is twelve. Eight plus twelve is

14
00:00:40,120 --> 00:00:44,167
twenty, exactly the same total. So five times four

15
00:00:44,167 --> 00:00:47,644
equals two times four, plus three times four. Two

16
00:00:47,644 --> 00:00:51,680
plus three, in a bracket, times four, gives the

17
00:00:51,680 --> 00:00:52,391
same split.

18
00:00:52,970 --> 00:00:55,390
Now let's see why that split works. The multiplier

19
00:00:55,390 --> 00:00:58,950
is four, and it stands outside the bracket. So

20
00:00:58,950 --> 00:01:01,770
four multiplies the first term, two; that gives two

21
00:01:01,770 --> 00:01:04,737
times four. And four also multiplies the second term,

22
00:01:04,737 --> 00:01:09,430
three; that gives three times four. Multiply every term

23
00:01:09,430 --> 00:01:12,481
inside the bracket, and eight plus twelve is twenty.

24
00:01:13,053 --> 00:01:15,813
We can write this rule with letters. The multiplier

25
00:01:15,813 --> 00:01:18,653
a reaches the first term, b, so we get

26
00:01:18,653 --> 00:01:21,450
a times b. And it reaches the second term,

27
00:01:21,450 --> 00:01:23,993
c, as well. So a times, b plus c,

28
00:01:23,993 --> 00:01:26,911
equals a b plus a c. That is the

29
00:01:26,911 --> 00:01:33,084
distributive property: multiply each term inside the bracket.

30
00:01:33,710 --> 00:01:36,077
Let's use it to make a big multiplication easier:

31
00:01:36,077 --> 00:01:40,850
twelve times seventy five. Now seventy five is seventy

32
00:01:40,850 --> 00:01:43,309
plus five. So twelve times seventy five is twelve

33
00:01:43,309 --> 00:01:48,590
times, seventy plus five, in a bracket. Now distribute:

34
00:01:48,590 --> 00:01:51,702
twelve times seventy is eight hundred and forty, and

35
00:01:51,702 --> 00:01:54,667
twelve times five is sixty. Eight hundred and forty

36
00:01:54,667 --> 00:01:58,147
plus sixty is nine hundred. A big multiplication became

37
00:01:58,147 --> 00:01:59,261
two easy ones!

38
00:01:59,877 --> 00:02:02,361
Now let's meet two special numbers, starting with zero.

39
00:02:02,361 --> 00:02:05,337
Here are seven counters, and next to them an

40
00:02:05,337 --> 00:02:09,697
empty container, which holds zero counters. When we add

41
00:02:09,697 --> 00:02:12,617
the empty container to the seven counters, the group

42
00:02:12,617 --> 00:02:14,917
does not change at all. Seven plus zero is

43
00:02:14,917 --> 00:02:19,997
seven. Zero is the additive identity, because adding zero

44
00:02:19,997 --> 00:02:21,948
leaves the number unchanged.

45
00:02:22,580 --> 00:02:25,220
Now, the number one. One group of seven counters

46
00:02:25,220 --> 00:02:27,480
is just seven. And those same seven counters can

47
00:02:27,480 --> 00:02:28,747
be shown as seven groups of one, which is

48
00:02:28,747 --> 00:02:32,360
still seven. So one times seven equals seven times

49
00:02:32,360 --> 00:02:37,720
one equals seven. One is the multiplicative identity. But

50
00:02:37,720 --> 00:02:42,300
be careful: multiplying by zero gives zero, not the

51
00:02:42,300 --> 00:02:43,591
original number.

52
00:02:44,090 --> 00:02:47,130
Here is a daily-life example. A milk vendor sells

53
00:02:47,130 --> 00:02:49,537
fifty six litres in the morning and forty four

54
00:02:49,537 --> 00:02:53,596
litres in the evening, at thirty rupees a litre.

55
00:02:53,596 --> 00:02:56,079
His earnings are fifty six times thirty, plus forty

56
00:02:56,079 --> 00:02:59,090
four times thirty. Both parts share the multiplier thirty,

57
00:02:59,090 --> 00:03:02,195
so we take it out: fifty six plus forty

58
00:03:02,195 --> 00:03:05,012
four, in a bracket, times thirty. That is one

59
00:03:05,012 --> 00:03:07,401
hundred times thirty, which is three thousand rupees.

60
00:03:07,987 --> 00:03:11,627
Your turn! Use the distributive property for five times,

61
00:03:11,627 --> 00:03:15,238
seven plus eight, in a bracket. Multiply five by

62
00:03:15,238 --> 00:03:18,740
each term inside the bracket. Pause the video and

63
00:03:18,740 --> 00:03:19,458
try it.

64
00:03:24,527 --> 00:03:27,187
Let's check. Five times seven is thirty five, and

65
00:03:27,187 --> 00:03:30,573
five times eight is forty. Thirty five plus forty

66
00:03:30,573 --> 00:03:35,267
is seventy five. And a quick identity check: nine

67
00:03:35,267 --> 00:03:37,199
plus zero is nine, and nine times one is

68
00:03:37,199 --> 00:03:38,118
also nine.

69
00:03:38,823 --> 00:03:42,919
Let's recap. The distributive property: a times, b plus

70
00:03:42,919 --> 00:03:45,923
c, equals a b plus a c. The additive

71
00:03:45,923 --> 00:03:48,710
identity is zero: a plus zero equals a. The

72
00:03:48,710 --> 00:03:54,663
multiplicative identity is one: a times one equals a.

73
00:03:54,663 --> 00:03:57,757
Distribute to simplify, and remember that the identities keep

74
00:03:57,757 --> 00:04:00,854
a number unchanged. Thank you for watching!
